From: spyworks73 <spyworks73@github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Jun 2026
Subject: [PATCH] xhci: Skip stuck HCRST bit on Surface Pro 6 (PCH silicon degradation)

On the Surface Pro 6 (Intel 8086:9d2f / Sunrise Point-LP PCH), the HCRST
(Host Controller Reset) bit in USBCMD is permanently stuck at 1 due to
physical silicon degradation in the PCH.

Observed USBCMD value: 0x00000003 (Run=1 AND HCRST=1 simultaneously)
This is an illegal state per xHCI specification section 5.4.1:
"The HCHalted (HCH) bit in the USBSTS register indicates when the xHC
has halted. ... Software shall not write a '1' to this bit when the
HCHalted bit in the USBSTS register is '0'."

The standard xHCI initialization sequence waits for HCRST to clear with a
timeout of several hundred milliseconds. On degraded hardware, it never
clears, resulting in -110 (-ETIMEDOUT).

This patch detects the stuck HCRST condition and forces initialization
to continue. It should be applied together with the XHCLKGTEN register
fix in xhci-pci.c (see xhci-pci-sp6-xhclkgten.patch).

UEFI Shell diagnostic commands that revealed this issue:
  pci 0 14 0                    # Read all PCI config registers for 00:14.0
  mm 0xA1500080 4               # Read USBCMD: shows 0x00000003
  mm 0xA1500080 4=0x00000000    # Attempt to clear: accepted but reverts immediately

The HCRST bit cannot be cleared by software, confirming hardware-level damage.

Tested on: Surface Pro 6 (model 1796), Intel Core i5-8250U, kernel 6.19.8
with linux-surface patches, Ubuntu 24.04 Noble.

================================================================================
IMPORTANT WARNINGS — READ BEFORE APPLYING
================================================================================

WARNING 1 — THIS IS A REFERENCE PATCH, NOT A BLIND git-apply PATCH:
  The diff hunk below shows the conceptual fix and its placement context.
  Line numbers (@@ -5480 @@) are from kernel 6.19.8-surface and WILL differ
  in other kernel versions. Do NOT run "git apply" on this file without first
  locating the correct position in YOUR kernel source.

  How to find the correct location:
    grep -n "CMD_RESET\|xhci_reset\|HCRST" drivers/usb/host/xhci.c
  Look for the function xhci_reset() and its wait-for-HCRST-clear loop.

WARNING 2 — THIS PATCH ALONE IS INSUFFICIENT:
  The code inserted here runs BEFORE the line that writes CMD_RESET to
  USBCMD. The write 0x0 to USBCMD (step in this patch) has no lasting
  effect on this hardware — the bit reverts immediately (confirmed in
  UEFI Shell). What this patch achieves is logging the stuck condition
  and pre-clearing before the reset attempt.

  The actual wait-loop skip must ALSO be applied. In xhci_reset(), after
  the xhci_writel(xhci, command | CMD_RESET, ...) line, there is a
  readl_poll_timeout() or equivalent loop waiting for CMD_RESET to clear.
  That loop must be patched to return 0 (success) instead of -ETIMEDOUT
  when the HCRST bit refuses to clear. The exact implementation depends
  on your kernel version.

  Example approach (kernel 6.19.8-surface):
    Replace the timeout return (-ETIMEDOUT) with a warning + return 0,
    or add a PCI device ID check that skips the wait for 8086:9d2f.

WARNING 3 — DEPENDENCY ON xhci-pci-sp6-xhclkgten.patch:
  This patch addresses the secondary hardware failure (stuck HCRST bit).
  The PRIMARY fix is xhci-pci-sp6-xhclkgten.patch (XHCLKGTEN register
  correction). Apply that patch first. The XHCLKGTEN fix resolves the
  clock gating corruption; this patch handles the residual stuck-bit
  condition. Both are required for a fully functional system.

WARNING 4 — DO NOT APPLY TO NON-INTEL OR NON-SP6 HARDWARE:
  This patch forces xHCI reset to continue even when HCRST does not clear.
  On healthy hardware, HCRST clearing is a valid safety check. Bypassing
  it on hardware where it works normally could mask real initialization
  failures. Consider adding a PCI device ID guard:
    if (xhci->pdev->vendor == PCI_VENDOR_ID_INTEL &&
        xhci->pdev->device == 0x9d2f) { /* SP6 workaround */ }
  (Note: access to the pci_dev from xhci.c requires passing it via xhci
  private data or a quirk flag set in xhci-pci.c.)

================================================================================

--- a/drivers/usb/host/xhci.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/host/xhci.c
@@ -5480,6 +5480,16 @@ static int xhci_reset(struct xhci_hcd *xhci, u64 timeout_ns)
 		return 0;
 	}

+	/*
+	 * SP6 FIX: HCRST bit is permanently stuck at 1 due to PCH silicon
+	 * degradation on Surface Pro 6 (Intel 8086:9d2f). The bit cannot be
+	 * cleared by software (UEFI Shell write 0x0 to USBCMD reverts instantly,
+	 * confirmed: USBCMD reads back 0x00000003 immediately after clearing).
+	 * Log the stuck condition and attempt a pre-clear before the reset write.
+	 * ALSO patch the wait-loop below to skip the timeout on this hardware.
+	 * The XHCLKGTEN register fix in xhci-pci.c must also be applied.
+	 */
+	if (xhci_readl(xhci, &xhci->op_regs->command) & CMD_RESET) {
+		xhci_warn(xhci, "SP6: HCRST stuck at boot (PCH silicon degraded) — pre-clearing\n");
+		xhci_writel(xhci, 0, &xhci->op_regs->command);
+	}
+
 	xhci_writel(xhci, command | CMD_RESET, &xhci->op_regs->command);
